#
# SYN's second use: the derived whole, actually made. NOT a merge that
# picks one anchor and discards the other — that would be EVA's job
# (judging between competing claims) applied where nothing here competes.
# turn-7's and turn-14's anchors are not competing; they are two true
# descriptions of related territory built from different content. SYN's
# job is to produce a THIRD entity that names the relation between them
# without erasing either.
#
# That third entity, minted and committed (kernel/data/anchor-004.json):
# a record whose content is `{relates: [both anchors, sorted], claim: "..."}`,
# hashed into its own anchor:
#
#   @446a13f9444ded9bc17694d79aa23af37acd40d90f738aecfdbb787faf63b8ab
#
# This is a genuine derived whole in the sense EO's definition requires:
# it exceeds either component (neither turn-7 nor turn-14 alone contains
# the CLAIM that relates them — that claim is new content, not a repackaging)
# and it is not reducible to concatenating the two inputs (a different
# ordering or a different claim about the same two anchors would produce
# a different digest, so the whole is sensitive to more than its parts'
# mere presence).
#
# Earning argument: precondition is turn-18 (a field admitting synthesis)
# and turn-7/turn-14 (the two anchors being synthesized). Refuted if the
# committed digest failed to reproduce from anchor-004.json's own content
# — independently recomputed, matches.

